  "boredom_engagement": "PARTIAL/INSTRUMENTAL -- boredom is one of three purpose-built experimental game-level conditions (alongside immersion and flow), measured via GEQ, MEC-SPQ, facial EMG, and GSR, but theorized only via a single borrowed definition (Fisher, 1993, p.83) and thereafter treated purely as 'the counterpart to engagement' / 'the lower end of a scale of engagement.' Flow is the paper's genuine theoretical center of gravity (Csikszentmihalyi's model, the four-channel revision, a dedicated GEQ subscale, extensive citation network), confirming the plan's expectation that 'flow / engagement (contrast pole)' would be strongly matched here, while the boredom-construct rows are populated thinly and flagged as such rather than inflated.",
  "thesis": "Lindley and Nacke report an experiment operationalizing and empirically differentiating boredom, immersion, and flow as three purpose-built level conditions within one game engine (three Half-Life 2 modifications), played by the same 25 participants, collecting both subjective (GEQ, MEC Spatial Presence Questionnaire) and objective (facial EMG for valence, galvanic skin response for arousal) data. The goal is to validate a method for precisely assessing real-time emotional modulation during gameplay and specifically to test whether the flow-designed level produces measurable high-arousal positive affect, as Csikszentmihalyi's theory predicts, rather than the frustration one might expect from steadily increasing combat difficulty. The experiment is explicitly framed as preliminary given the still-underdeveloped state of psychophysiological characterization of flow/immersion.",
  "sections": [
    { "title": "Abstract, Keywords, 1. Introduction incl. 1.1 Immersion", "page": "p.81", "summary": "Motivates the study from the lack of a formal taxonomy for flow/immersion/boredom/excitement/challenge/fun; introduces the FPS-genre rationale (player-as-character via 'virtual prostheses,' Grimshaw); surveys Brown & Cairns's engagement/engrossment/total-immersion gradient and Ermi & Mäyrä's sensory/challenge-based/imaginative immersion typology (SCI model), which ranked Half-Life 2 highest on all dimensions." },
    { "title": "1.2 Flow; 1.3 Psychophysiological measurements", "page": "p.82-83", "summary": "Defines flow via Csikszentmihalyi (1975, 1990); presents Ellis et al.'s four-channel model (Fig.1: flow/anxiety/apathy/boredom quadrants against skill x challenge); surveys Chen's flow zones, Novak et al.'s finding of 16 divergent flow-study definitions (1977-1996), the Flow State Scale, and Kivikangas's null EMG-flow finding; introduces facial EMG (valence) and GSR (arousal) via Lang's dimensional theory of emotion." },
    { "title": "2. Method (2.1 Design incl. 2.1.1 Boredom/2.1.2 Immersion/2.1.3 Flow criteria; 2.2 Participants; 2.3 Apparatus; 2.4 Procedure; 2.5 Data reduction)", "page": "p.83-86", "summary": "Gives concrete level-design criteria per condition (boredom: linear, repetitive, no surprises, no real winning condition; immersion: exploratory, sensory-rich, narrative left out; flow: single-weapon mechanic, gradually increasing combat challenge, cooldown rest spots); reports N=25 male hardcore-gamer sample; details EMG/GSR/video/GEQ apparatus plus an unanalyzed 32-channel EEG system and Tobii 1750 eye tracker; describes fixed condition order (no counterbalancing), 2-hour sessions, BESA/SPSS analysis pipeline." },
    { "title": "3. Results", "page": "p.86-87", "summary": "GEQ (Fig.3) discriminates only challenge and tension across conditions; MEC-SPQ (Table 1) discriminates both self-location and possible-actions, immersion level highest; EMG/GSR (Table 2) discriminates OO, ZM, and GSR (flow highest on all three) but not CS; within-subject contrasts show flow > boredom (OO, ZM, GSR) and flow > immersion (ZM only)." },
    { "title": "4. Discussion and Future Work; 5. Acknowledgments; References", "page": "p.87-88", "summary": "Interprets flow's high-arousal positive-valence profile as evidence that challenge, not victory, produces joy; explicitly contradicts Kivikangas's (2006) null EMG-flow finding; concedes sample/generalizability and fixed-order limitations; proposes finer time-resolution analysis and analysis of the deferred EEG/eye-tracking data as future work; 29 references." }
  ],
